        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-queneau_poems_are_ten_to_the_fourteen",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "A HUNDRED THOUSAND BILLION POEMS: 14 positions, each filled independently by one of 10 interchangeable strips, give 10^14 = 100000000000000 sonnets — the title's number, counted."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "10 ^ 14 = 100000000000000"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem queneau_poems_are_ten_to_the_fourteen : 10 ^ 14 = 100000000000000 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-queneau_one_strip_per_position",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE CHOICES MULTIPLY BECAUSE THEY ARE INDEPENDENT: the rhyme sound at a position is shared by all 10 strips, so choosing one position never constrains another, and the count is the product of 14 factors of 10 — the power, written out as the product it is."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(List.replicate 14 10).foldl (· * ·) 1 = 10 ^ 14"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem queneau_one_strip_per_position : (List.replicate 14 10).foldl (· * ·) 1 = 10 ^ 14 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-queneau_poems_are_the_fourteen_digit_numerals",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "EVERY POEM IS A NUMERAL: which sonnet supplies line i is one decimal digit, so a poem is a 14-digit decimal numeral and the poems are the numerals below 10^14 — the largest poem is 99999999999999, all nines, the last strip at every position."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "100000000000000 - 1 = 99999999999999 ∧ 100000000000000 = 10 ^ 14"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem queneau_poems_are_the_fourteen_digit_numerals : 100000000000000 - 1 = 99999999999999 ∧ 100000000000000 = 10 ^ 14 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-queneau_a_sonnet_is_eight_and_six",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "THE SONNET'S OWN SHAPE: 14 lines are the octave and the sestet, 8 + 6, so the poems factor as 10^8 · 10^6 — every octave from the book can meet every sestet."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "8 + 6 = 14 ∧ 10 ^ 8 * 10 ^ 6 = 10 ^ 14"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem queneau_a_sonnet_is_eight_and_six : 8 + 6 = 14 ∧ 10 ^ 8 * 10 ^ 6 = 10 ^ 14 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-queneau_ten_sonnets_are_a_vanishing_sample",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "WHAT THE BOOK PRINTS AGAINST WHAT IT HOLDS: 10 complete sonnets are printed, and they are 10 of 100000000000000 — one in ten trillion; the other 99999999999990 exist only as choices."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "100000000000000 / 10 = 10000000000000 ∧ 10 < 100000000000000"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem queneau_ten_sonnets_are_a_vanishing_sample : 100000000000000 / 10 = 10000000000000 ∧ 10 < 100000000000000 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
